    25	## Strict-threshold nonattainment
    26	Construction: \(T=2\), deterministic \(P_2=1\).
    27	Facts: For the first quote, \(r_1(a)=a{\bf 1}\{a<1\}\). The supremum over \(a\in[0,1]\) is \(1\), but no maximizer exists.
    28	Why it matters: Final statements should use \(\sup_a\), not \(\max_a\), unless extra attainment assumptions are imposed.
    29	Status: Valid stress test.
    30	Source: `iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md` and critique.
    31	
    32	## Grid/continuum mismatch in finite-grid identities
    33	Construction: \(T=2\), singleton law with deterministic \(P_2=3/4\), and quote grid \(G=\{0,1/2\}\).
    34	Facts: The grid comparator value is \(V^G=1/2\), but the continuum comparator supremum is \(3/4\). Thus an exact finite-grid minimax identity using continuum \(V\) or a continuum Bayes envelope is off by \(1/4\).
    35	Why it matters: Finite-grid theorems must restrict learner actions, comparator, and Bayes predictive envelope to the same grid. Passing to \([0,1]\) requires a separate discretization error.
    36	Status: Valid stress test.
    37	Source: `iterations/iter_004/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
    38	
    39	## Randomization is necessary for minimax/Bayes identities
    40	Construction: \(T=2\), \(P_1=0\), grid \(G=\{0.5,0.9\}\). Two worlds: in world 0, \(P_2=0.6\); in world 1, \(P_2=1\).
    41	Facts: The learner cannot distinguish worlds before choosing its only payoff-relevant quote. A deterministic learner choosing \(0.5\) has regrets \((0,0.4)\), and choosing \(0.9\) has regrets \((0.5,0)\), so deterministic minimax regret is \(0.4\). Randomizing with probability \(5/9\) on \(0.5\) balances regrets at \(2/9\).
    42	Why it matters: Least-favorable-prior/minimax equalities must allow randomized policies or mixed kernels.
    43	Status: Valid finite-grid stress test.
    44	Source: `iterations/iter_004/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
    45	
    46	## Adaptive known-law policies can have negative regret
    47	Construction: \(T=2\), grid \(G=\{0.5,0.9\}\). Let \(P_1\in\{0,1\}\) with probability \(1/2\) each; set \(P_2=0.6\) if \(P_1=0\), and \(P_2=1\) if \(P_1=1\). The learner knows the law and observes \(P_1\) before quoting.
    48	Facts: The best fixed grid quote has value \(0.5\). The adaptive known-law learner quotes \(0.5\) after \(P_1=0\) and \(0.9\) after \(P_1=1\), receiving expected reward \((0.5+0.9)/2=0.7\). Its regret against the best fixed quote is \(-0.2\).
    49	Why it matters: "Sublinear regret" should be stated as an upper-regret/positive-part requirement, not as absolute convergence of a minimax value to zero.
    50	Status: Valid sign-convention stress test.
    51	Source: `iterations/iter_004/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
    52	
    53	## Known terminal spike refutes necessity of \(\Delta\)
    54	Construction: Horizon \(T\). Let \(P_1=\cdots=P_{T-1}=0\), \(P_T\sim\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]\), and suppose the learner knows this singleton law.
    55	Facts: For each early quote, expected reward is \(a(1-a)\), maximized at \(a=1/2\). The learner can quote \(1/2\) and get zero regret. For any \(H=o(T)\), most early rewards are excluded by truncation, so \(\Delta_T(H)\approx (T-H)/4=\Omega(T)\).
    56	Why it matters: Large truncation loss can be harmless when the delayed reward curve is known/predictable.
    57	Status: Valid obstruction to \(\Delta\) as a necessary condition.
    58	Source: idea_01 explorer and critique.
    59	
    60	## Hidden-terminal minimax lower-bound instance
    61	Construction: Horizon \(T=D+1\). Two worlds share \(P_1=\cdots=P_D=0\). At terminal time,
    62	\[
    63	Y_L\sim\mathrm{Unif}[1/2,5/8],\qquad
    64	Y_H\sim\mathrm{Unif}[3/4,7/8].
    65	\]
    66	Facts: Before terminal time the learner cannot distinguish worlds. Per early quote \(a\), \(g_i(a)=a\Pr(Y_i>a)\) has \(V_L=1/2\), \(V_H=3/4\), while the best Bayes action under a uniform prior has value \(1/2\). Bayes gap is \(1/8\) per early round.
    67	Why it matters: Shows long delayed rewards can force \(\Omega(D)\) regret for a universal/minimax learner over an unknown class.
    68	Status: Valid finite-horizon class-level lower bound; not a fixed-law necessity result.
    69	Source: idea_02 explorer and critique.
    70	
    71	## q-scaled hidden-terminal lower-bound atom
    72	Construction: Horizon \(T=D+1\). Two worlds share \(P_1=\cdots=P_D=0\). At terminal time, world \(L\) uses \((1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[1/2,5/8]\), while world \(H\) uses \((1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[3/4,7/8]\). For infinite-process tail calculations, set \(P_s=0\) for \(s>T\).
    73	Facts: Every learner has regret at least \(qD/8\) on one of the two worlds. With absorbing extension, \(\beta(h)=q\) for \(h<D\) and \(0\) for \(h\ge D\). For integer \(H<D\),
    74	\[
    75	\Delta_L(H)=\delta_L(H)=q(D-H)/2,\qquad
    76	\Delta_H(H)=\delta_H(H)=3q(D-H)/4.
    77	\]
    78	Why it matters: This is the cleanest quantitative lower-bound atom linking valuable delayed tail mass and indistinguishability.
    79	Status: Valid finite-horizon minimax lower bound. It is not a singleton known-law obstruction, and by itself it does not match power-tail upper rates.
    80	Source: `iterations/iter_003/idea_02/explorer_01.md` and critique.
    81	
    82	## One-block power-tail lower-bound weakness
    83	Construction idea: Use the q-scaled atom under a nonincreasing tail envelope \(B\) by choosing \(q\le B(D)\).
    84	Facts: The resulting lower bound is only \(\Omega(D B(D))\). For \(B(D)=D^{-\alpha}\), this is \(\Omega(D^{1-\alpha})\), sublinear in the wrong exponent and non-growing for \(\alpha\ge1\).
    85	Why it matters: Shows the q-scaled atom is not enough for rate matching. Repetition must avoid iid block-splicing collapse and global-type one-reveal learning.
    86	Status: Valid obstruction to a proposed rate-matching route.
    87	Source: `iterations/iter_003/idea_02/explorer_01.md` and critique.
    88	
    89	## Rare bad histories can break essential sup without large average loss
    90	Construction idea: Arrange histories with very small unconditional probability where a long delayed hit is certain.
    91	Facts needed: Essential supremum over histories can keep \(\beta(H)\) large, while \(\Delta_T(H)/T\) remains small because the histories almost never occur.
    92	Why it matters: Would show strict weakness of average truncation criteria compared with \(\beta\).
    93	Status: Plausible; needs explicit process and estimates.
    94	Source: idea_01 critique.
    95	
    96	## Tiny-price long-delay blocks may break raw beta without meaningful regret
    97	Construction idea: Deterministic or stochastic blocks with very long zero runs followed by prices \(p_m\downarrow0\), with block lengths increasing.
    98	Facts needed: For every \(h\), some block creates a conditional state with \(\Pr(h<\tau<\infty)=1\), so \(\beta(h)=1\). But if \(p_m\) decreases fast enough, total best fixed comparator value may be \(o(T)\), making sublinear regret trivial.
    99	Why it matters: Raw delay probability ignores reward magnitude.
   100	Status: Plausible; parameter choices must be formalized.
   101	Source: idea_02 explorer and critique.
   102	
   103	## Action-dependent prices obstruct full-information reduction
   104	Construction idea: Let future prices depend on the learner's current or future quotes.
   105	Facts: Then counterfactual rewards \(r_t^H(b)\) for unplayed grid quotes may not be determined by the realized future price path, or may correspond to a different path under a comparator policy.
   106	Why it matters: The upper-bound theorem is not valid in this model without new causal definitions.
   107	Status: Model obstruction, not a numeric counterexample yet.
   108	Source: idea_01 critique.
   109	
   110	## Candidate anytime obstruction via two deadlines
   111	Construction idea: Deterministic known infinite sequence with super-growing blocks: \(N_m\) zeros, then price \(1/2\), then \(N_m\) zeros, then price \(1\), with \(N_m\) dominating all previous lengths.
   112	Facts sketched by critic: For the first \(N_m\) quotes of such a pair, the benchmark by the early deadline is about \(N_m/2\), while the benchmark by the later deadline is about \(N_m\). For any horizon-free quotes \(a_i\),
   113	\[
   114	A=N_m/2-\sum_i a_i{\bf 1}\{a_i<1/2\},\qquad
   115	B=N_m-\sum_i a_i
   116	\]
   117	satisfy \(A+B\ge N_m/2\), so one of the two deadlines has regret at least \(N_m/4\) from that block.
   118	Why it matters: The known-law oracle lemma depends on knowing the evaluation horizon. A single anytime policy may not inherit the \(O(1)\) guarantee.
   119	Status: Plausible stress test; needs a fully formal infinite-process and horizon-subsequence proof.
   120	Source: `iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.

